\par
\tab Note: All of the options stated above assume the /W option switch \tab is not set to allow binary file loading.\par
\par
\tab If the chain to P?S/8 BIN is for the purpose of punching binary \tab paper-tapes, the following option switches apply:\par
\par
\tab /W\tab The /G option switch is set; the chain to P?S/8 BIN will be \tab\tab used to punch binary paper-tapes if there are no assembly \tab\tab\tab errors (else the chain to BIN will be prevented).\par
\par
\tab /F\tab The default device for the binary paper-tape output is the \tab\tab\tab low-speed console device paper-tape punch found on various \tab\tab\tab console terminals such as the Teletype Model 33 ASR and \tab\tab\tab Model 35 ASR (device 04).  If the /F option switch is set, \tab\tab\tab the binary output is directed to the high-speed punch \tab\tab\tab\tab (device 02).\par
\par
\tab /R\tab By default, the paper-tape output is punched in DEC \i BIN\i0\par
 \tab\tab format.  If the /R option switch is set, the output format\par
\tab\tab used is enhanced DEC \i RIM\i0  format (which includes a checksum \tab\tab\tab so the binary paper-tape can also be read by the \i BIN\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab loader).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: the /0 option switch is not passed to P?S/8 BIN as it\par
\tab\tab has alternate meaning within P?S/8 PAL.  See Section 2.50\par
\tab\tab for more details.\page\ul 2.40\tab Text management options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /J\tab Prevent generation of a 12-bit word of 0000 after text \tab\tab\tab strings with an even count of characters as used with the \tab\tab\tab TEXT and SIXBIT directives.\par
\par
\ul 2.41 Text directive issues\ulnone\par
\par
\tab\tab The PAL language defines the TEXT directive which is used \tab\tab\tab with a string argument.  The string consists of an \tab\tab\tab\tab indefinite number of printable characters preceded by a \tab\tab\tab delimiter character which cannot be included in the \tab\tab\tab\tab generated text; the string ends with a second usage of the \tab\tab\tab delimiter\tab character.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: P?S/8 PAL does not require the trailing delimiter \tab\tab\tab character unless there are multiple statements on the same \tab\tab\tab line.\par
\par
\tab\tab Early implementations of the TEXT directive restricted the \tab\tab\tab delimiter character to \highlight3 "\highlight0 , \highlight3 '\highlight0  and in some cases \highlight3 @\highlight0  (which is \tab\tab\tab not supported by P?S/8 PAL and other assemblers).  The use \tab\tab\tab of \highlight3 "\highlight0  or \highlight3 '\highlight0  as text delimiters is recommended for maximum \tab\tab\tab compatibility with other PAL assemblers; P?S/8 PAL and OS/8 \tab\tab PAL8 support an extension allowing any printing character \tab\tab\tab not contained within the text string to be used as the \tab\tab\tab delimiter character.\par
\par
\tab\tab All usage of the TEXT directive creates strings of six-bit \tab\tab\tab ASCII text characters packed two per 12-bit word.  If the \tab\tab\tab count of characters in the string is an odd number, the last \tab\tab 12-bit word has 00 in the low-order six bits which can act \tab\tab\tab as a string delimiter.\par
\par
\tab\tab Since the most likely usage of the TEXT directive is to \tab\tab\tab generate strings used with printing subroutines, the default \tab\tab action for string arguments with even character counts is to \tab\tab output an additional 12-bit word of 0000 to ensure the \tab\tab\tab presence of a string delimiter.\par
\par
\tab\tab TEXT directive usage is not limited to string creation as \tab\tab\tab described above; some alternate schemes use internal \tab\tab\tab\tab printing characters as delimiters or other control purposes.  \tab\tab Within the P?S/8 keyboard monitor coding, explicit string \tab\tab\tab length arguments are used in some instances; in other cases \tab\tab the string length is an implicit constant value.  The 00 \tab\tab\tab case plays no role in any of these cases; creation of an \tab\tab\tab extra 12-bit word of 0000 is undesirable.\page\tab\tab Note: Early PDP-8 programs such as \ul FOCAL, 1969\ulnone  were \tab\tab\tab\tab developed on systems incapable of modifying the output of \tab\tab\tab the TEXT directive; as such, the source code includes \tab\tab\tab\tab statements to backup the assembler current location counter \tab\tab to allow the desired contents which differs from the \tab\tab\tab\tab automatically generated 0000 value.  Ironically, the \tab\tab\tab\tab source-level workaround is incompatible with the /J option \tab\tab\tab switch which would eliminate the problem.\par
\par
\tab\tab As documented above, setting the /J option switch prevents \tab\tab\tab the creation of the extra 12-bit word of 0000 on even count \tab\tab text strings.  It is strongly recommended the disposition of \tab\tab the /J option switch be documented at the beginning of all \tab\tab\tab source code; in many cases, this may be the only departure \tab\tab\tab from assembler default settings and could easily be \tab\tab\tab\tab overlooked.\par
\par
\ul 2.42 Other text-related issues\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL supports the SIXBIT directive which takes the same general form as the TEXT directive.  The only difference is the internal six-bit code used is not the PDP-8 standard six-bit subset of the ASCII character set; instead, the alternate definition of six-bit text as used on DEC 18-bit and 36-bit computers applies.\par
\par
The basic difference between the two character sets is the highest of the six bits used is inverted.  Input processing of seven-bit ASCII characters to six-bit code is easier using the PDP-8 standard while output processing of six-bit code to seven-bit ASCII characters is easier using the alternative standard.  The PDP-8 standard was chosen largely because there are often multiple input routines while output is generally performed by a single output routine.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 does not support the SIXBIT directive.\par
\par
The DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives are supported by P?S/8 PAL and PAL8.  These are special-purpose text directives to create a four-character and eight-character text string respectively.  Early OS/8 programs required the use of the /J option switch (or its equivalent in PAL8 which is the /F option switch); this allows creation of the intended string arguments while preventing compatibility with the usual string printing applications, which require even-length string termination.  With the availability of the DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives, the normal flexibility of text string creation is restored.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports the FILENA\{ME\} directive for compatibility purposes only.  Future P?S/8 systems will support the P?S/8 SHELL with an extended assembler based on P?S/8 PAL to be known as \i XPAL\i0 .  XPAL will support an option to extend the FILENA\{ME\} directive to support SHELL directory file names.\page Certain user-modified assemblers exist that support the \i TEXTZ\i0  directive which forces the extra 12-bit word of 0000 even if the /J option switch (or equivalent) is in effect.  Future versions of P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  may support the TEXTZ directive and the \i SIXBTZ\i0  directive (using the alternate six-bit text code).\par
\par
Note: As of PAL8 Version B0 (the last version released by DEC), the TEXTZ directive is not supported.\par
\par
Most PAL assemblers (including P?S/8 PAL and PAL8) support the \highlight3 "\highlight0  operator in expressions.  The next character (which cannot be \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0 ) is used to form the seven-bit ASCII value of the stated character; the mark parity bit is set.\par
\par
A proposed \i ASCII\i0  directive would take a string argument much like the TEXT directive to create a string of \ul seven-bit ASCII\ulnone  characters.  The /J option switch would prevent a trailing 12-bit word of 0000.\par
\par
An additional \i ASCIIZ\i0  directive could be implemented to force the creation of the trailing 12-bit word of 0000 regardless of the setting of the /J option switch.\par
\par
Note: An additional issue is whether to have an option to prevent the mark parity bit within the text string as arithmetic operations are not possible on individual string characters.\par
\par
Even without any form of \i ASCII\i0  (or similar) directive, it is currently possible to create \ul seven-bit ASCII\ulnone  text strings with any of the discussed variations using the \highlight3 "\highlight0  operator.  When used alone, each character in the string will have the mark parity set; string delimiter characters can be added if desired, such as ending the string with a 12-bit word of 0000 or making the last character of the string negative (in the 12-bit sense).  By adding an additional element in each expression, it is possible to strip off the mark parity bit:\par
\par
HLOWRLD,"H&177;"E&177;"L&177;"L&177;"O&177;" &177\tab /HELLO WORLD MESSAGE\par
        "W&177;"O&177;"R&177;"L&177;"D&177!4000\tab /NEGATIVE ENDS TEXT.\par
\par
An unnamed additional text directive has been proposed to create packed seven-bit ASCII text strings following the \ul\i three for two\i0  \ulnone packing convention as used in OS/8 text files (and the P?S/8 SHELL).  Because of alignment restrictions, additional null eight-bit bytes will be required, regardless of the issue regarding the mark parity bit.  If the string is meant to be an image of an actual file section, there must also be a mechanism to append a trailing \highlight3 Control-Z\highlight0  character into the string.\par
\par
Note: The issue of a \ul three for two\ulnone  directive will likely be taken up in the XPAL assembler which will support upper-case/lower-case input files.  A \i PARITY\i0  directive with appropriate argument support can be implemented to handle all mark parity bit (and perhaps \highlight3 Control-Z\highlight0  character) issues raised above.\page\ul 2.50\tab Additional option switches and issues\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /0\tab Automatically generate *200 after the use of the FIELD \tab\tab\tab directive.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Most programs have data or explicit origins after \tab\tab\tab FIELD settings.  By using an explicit origin setting, a \tab\tab\tab known serious bug of one of the released DEC BIN loaders \tab\tab\tab (known as the \ul\i self-starting\ulnone\i0  binary loader) can be avoided.\par
\par
\tab\tab The problem with this version of the loader (which was never \tab\tab fixed) is that when a FIELD setting is encountered, it is \tab\tab\tab applied prematurely; the last data word from the previous \tab\tab\tab address (presumably intended for use with the former FIELD \tab\tab\tab setting in effect) is erroneously loaded into the new field.\par
\tab\tab By ensuring the last data frame is an origin setting, the \tab\tab\tab problem is generally prevented.\par
\par
\tab\tab As a practical matter, most well-designed programs use \tab\tab\tab specific origins after FIELD settings for appropriate \tab\tab\tab\tab purposes; as such, this option is generally irrelevant.\par
\par
\tab /Y\tab The \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator is changed from inclusive OR (.OR) to shift\par
 \tab\tab left six bits.\par
\par
\tab\tab Without the /Y option switch set, 22!33 generates 0033; with \tab\tab the /Y option switch set, 22!33 generates 2233.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator (with the /Y option switch set) can be used \tab\tab\tab to create short six-bit text expressions:\par
\par
\tab\tab A=\tab "A&77\par
\par
\tab\tab B=\tab "B&77\par
\par
\tab\tab A!B\par
\par
\tab\tab Each word created using the \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator in this manner \tab\tab\tab\tab produces the same result as a 12-bit component (two \tab\tab\tab\tab characters) of a text string.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Certain programs require the default \highlight3 !\highlight0  when \tab\tab\tab\tab embellishing combined OPR or IOT instructions:\par
\par
\tab\tab CLA!CLL!CML!RTL\tab\tab /SET THE ACCUMULATOR TO 0002.\par
\par
\tab\tab DTSF!DTRB\tab\tab\tab\tab /SKIP ON DECTAPE DONE OR ERROR FLAG\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /SET; READ THE ERROR REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab\tab Programmer preference will determine whether to use the /Y \tab\tab\tab option switch.  As in the case of the /J option switch, if \tab\tab\tab the /Y option switch should be set, this should be \tab\tab\tab\tab documented in the beginning of the source code to ensure \tab\tab\tab proper assembly.\page\ul 2.60\tab P?S/8 PAL directives\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is fully compatible with all standard directives as implemented in OS/8 PAL8.  Certain additional directives are supported compatible with other assemblers such as the \i DIAL-MS \i0 embedded assembler or TOPS-10 PAL10.  Additional directives are unique to P?S/8 PAL.  There are special issues with regard to the \i DTORG\i0  directive as discussed below.\par
\par
Note: A complete list of P?S/8 PAL directives is provided in Appendix B below.\par
\par
\ul 2.61 Issues regarding the DTORG directive\ulnone\par
\par
The OS/8 implementation of the DTORG directive is deliberately avoided in P?S/8 PAL.  A proposed solution to the underlying problem is presented below which will allow the DTORG directive to be useful to P?S/8 system generation in future releases of P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\ul 2.62 OS/8 PAL8 implementation of DTORG\ulnone\par
\par
As implemented in PAL8, the DTORG directive is provided for compatibility with PAL10.  The DTORG directive is meant to create 128 words/block numbered block references within the binary output to allow a special-purpose loader (only used within the DEC \i Typeset-8\i0  group) to write Typeset-8 system blocks on DECtape (and perhaps other devices).\par
\par
The general function is to specify a numbered storage block on a TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape followed by the (up to) 128 words to be written to the specified block.\par
\par
The origin in memory is set prior to the DTORG directive; however, it is generally ignored because no form of standard loader (such as the BIN paper-tape binary loader) is supported.  The origin is used to define the upper limit in memory of the (up to) 128 data words that follow; once an entire PDP-8 page has been defined (placing the current location counter on the next PDP-8 page), the intended block contents are finalized.\line\line Typeset-8 programs create a series of these small sections; each is loaded by a special-purpose Typeset-8 generation utility which writes the data to the specified DECtape (or other device) logical block.  However, there are serious issues:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab Each section written to a targeted DECtape block is taken \tab\tab\tab from a lengthy punched binary paper-tape created at an OS/8 \tab\tab development site; the tape invariably was the latest release \tab\tab of a major section of the Typeset operating system (or one \tab\tab\tab of its utilities).  In-house Typeset-8 machines generally\par
\tab\tab did not include high-speed readers; these systems invariably\par
\tab\tab had Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals to read in the binary\par
\tab\tab data, etc.\page\tab b)\tab There is no form of checksum protection associated with the \tab\tab written data.  Since the binary paper-tapes were generally \tab\tab\tab quite long, this is patently foolhardy.\par
\par
\tab\tab By the nature of paper-tape, it is expected that reading in \tab\tab long binary paper-tapes will occasionally result in read \tab\tab\tab errors.  This is why the BIN loader format includes checksum \tab\tab frames.  \par
\par
\tab\tab The RIM loader, which lacks a checksum mechanism, is only \tab\tab\tab used to load short binary programs such as the BIN loader, \tab\tab\tab which in turn can load in significant binary program data \tab\tab\tab with the confidence provided by passing an overall checksum \tab\tab test.\par
\par
\tab c)\tab Despite being created by PAL8 in OS/8 standard binary \tab\tab\tab\tab format (as a .BN file), the binary output violates the OS/8 \tab\tab binary file standard; the output of a program containing \tab\tab\tab instances of the DTORG directive cannot be loaded\par
\tab\tab successfully by OS/8 ABSLDR.  Attempts to read in the \tab\tab\tab\tab punched binary paper-tape will always end in a checksum\par
\tab\tab error, even if an error-free read was performed.  This is\par
\tab\tab because the implementation of the DTORG directive usurps the\par
\tab\tab underlying BIN format that OS/8 converts into \ul three-for-two \ulnone\par
\tab\tab format OS/8 records (384 frames per logical record).\par
\par
\tab\tab As such, it is impossible to improve the performance of the \tab\tab Typeset-8 special-purpose loader; reading in the entire \tab\tab\tab binary paper-tape will not yield valid checksum information \tab\tab that could be used to reject attempts to read the lengthy \tab\tab\tab tapes that introduce additional flaws.\par
\par
\ul 2.63 Proposed remedies to the DTORG problem\ulnone\par
\par
Apparently due to internal DEC politics, no attempt was made to remedy this problem by educating the Typeset-8 programmers regarding their naive design; various well-known mechanisms can easily solve the problem.\par
\par
\tab a)\tab Instead of implementing the DTORG directive as a violation \tab\tab\tab of the internal binary format of BIN utilities and OS/8, the \tab\tab binary data can be patterned after a variety of utility \tab\tab\tab formats such as certain details of P?S/8 non-system device \tab\tab\tab handlers and OS/8 device handlers (as required by OS/8 \tab\tab\tab\i BUILD\i0 ).\page\tab\tab An arbitrary series of origin settings can be used to \tab\tab\tab\tab indicate the intention of DTORG directive statements as \tab\tab\tab follows:\par
\par
          DTORG  1234               /DESIGNATE BLOCK 1234 FOR OUTPUT.\par
\par
\tab\tab The DTORG usage could create the equivalent of the following \tab\tab binary output:\par
\par
          PREV=  .                  /NEED THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN SETTING.\par
\par
          *7777;*1234;*PREV         /OUTPUT FLAG ORIGIN FOLLOWED BY\par
                                    /THE DTORG BLOCK NUMBER FOLLOWED\par
                                    /BY THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab\tab A special-purpose loader is still required to create\par
\tab\tab Typset-8 system blocks in the proposed format; however, the \tab\tab binary data created is compatible with all loading \tab\tab\tab\tab mechanisms; a lengthy binary paper-tape punched out and then \tab\tab read on a Teletype Model 35 ASR read station could be known \tab\tab to have passed the checksum test.  Afterwards, all \tab\tab\tab\tab designated blocks can be written out at once.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The Typeset-8 system runs on 4K machines.  The binary\par
\tab\tab paper tapes used would tend to be somewhat longer than the\par
\tab\tab binary paper-tape for \ul FOCAL, 1969\ulnone  as a representative \tab\tab\tab\tab example.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab The example remedy represents the most expedient method to \tab\tab\tab solve the DTORG problem.  More elaborate schemes could \tab\tab\tab include additional origin settings, perhaps following the \tab\tab\tab last two data-based origins as shown in the example.  An \tab\tab\tab overall mini-checksum for the entire string of origin \tab\tab\tab\tab settings could be created if desired.\par
\par
\tab\tab Regardless of implementation details, the entire problem of \tab\tab supporting incompatible binary files would be eliminated.  \tab\tab\tab All binary files can be used with all available binary \tab\tab\tab format utilities.  After punching the lengthy paper-tape, it \tab\tab could be read back in using the high-speed paper-tape \tab\tab\tab\tab reader.  The resulting file would not only have to pass the \tab\tab standard checksum test, but additionally could be directly \tab\tab\tab compared to the original binary output file to completely \tab\tab\tab confirm the validity of the paper-tape before bring the tape \tab\tab to the Typeset-8 back-room facility for the usual purpose of\par
\tab\tab generating Typeset-8 system components.\par
\par
\tab c)\tab Without having to support incompatible binary format, the \tab\tab\tab need to store paper-tape binary frames becomes superfluous.  \tab\tab OS/8 could have easily been developed using a more efficient \tab\tab internal file format similar to P?S/8 and the \ul\i R-L Monitor\ulnone\i0\par
\tab\tab\ul\i System\ulnone\i0 .\page\tab\tab It is well known that DEC managers perverted certain aspects \tab\tab of the demo project that was eventually to become OS/8. \tab\tab\tab Their demonstrable ignorance of PDP-8 operating systems has \tab\tab left its mark on various forms of internal poor design \tab\tab\tab aspects of this system.\par
\par
\tab\tab It is surmised that Richard Lary intended to use Slurp \tab\tab\tab binary format for OS/8 (having just recently demonstrated \tab\tab\tab the R-L Monitor to the same dim bulbs who rejected it), but \tab\tab the edict to use images of binary paper-tape frames clearly \tab\tab was created in part due to demands of the Typeset-8 group.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL10 also implements the creation of\tab  incompatible\par
\tab\tab binary paper-tapes; the DTORG suppor is dentical to that of\par
\tab\tab PAL8.\par
\par
\tab d)\tab OS/8 \i PIP\i0  could easily be modified to convert compatible \tab\tab\tab binary files using DTORG directives (as proposed above) \tab\tab\tab into Typeset-8 format binary paper-tapes (assuming no \tab\tab\tab\tab cooperation from the Typeset-8 group was forthcoming).\par
\tab\tab Thus, the problems associated with incompatible binary\par
\tab\tab format would only affect the users of Typeset-8, and not the\par
\tab\tab entire PDP-8 community at large.\par
\par
\tab\tab While not likely to occur, the binary format of OS/8 \tab\tab\tab\tab utilities could be updated to \i Slurp\i0  binary format which \tab\tab\tab would improve the performance of OS/8 for all users.\par
\par
\tab e)\tab While the DTORG directive is not particularly useful for \tab\tab\tab OS/8 purposes (just about every aspect of the present DTORG \tab\tab implementation is responsible for some negative aspect of \tab\tab\tab OS/8 performance) since it addresses 128 logical words/block\par
\tab\tab storage (while OS/8 addresses 256 word logical records), it\par
\tab\tab can be useful for future implementation of P?S/8 system\par
\tab\tab generation utilities that follow the overall flow of the\par
\tab\tab Typeset-8 system generation method (without any of the\par
\tab\tab flawed details).\par
\par
\tab\tab Future releases of P?S/8 PAL (and the SHELL assembler \i XPAL\i0 ) \tab\tab will develop a more useful implementation of the DTORG \tab\tab\tab directive; for the present, P?S/8 PAL merely implements \tab\tab\tab DTORG as one of the nonfunctional directives supported for \tab\tab\tab source-level compatibility reasons, but generates an \tab\tab\tab\tab incompatible subset of the proposed changes.\par
\par
\tab f)\tab A P?S/8 utility, tentatively to be known as \i BINCON\i0 , may be \tab\tab\tab written to read and convert present OS/8 binary files to the \tab\tab proposed format (with respect to the DTORG disposition; the \tab\tab resulting compatible binary format would then be stored as \tab\tab\tab P?S/8 Slurp binary format files.  This would implement \tab\tab\tab binary compatibility with the proposed changes to DTORG \tab\tab\tab as implemented in future releases of P?S/8 PAL.  P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab system generation utilities could operate with binary paper \tab\tab tapes or other media developed on either operating system.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: P?S/8 BIN can create binary paper tapes in DEC BIN \tab\tab\tab format on the low-speed or high-speed punch; this can be\par
 \tab\tab accomplished by a chained \ul assemble-and-punch\ulnone  operation as \tab\tab\tab described\tab elsewhere in this document.\page\ul 2.70 Additional information\ulnone\par
\par
The reader is directed to various documents available on the Internet covering the fundamentals of PDP-8 programming and the PAL language as implemented on various systems (not all of which are compatible with DEC standards).  P?S/8 PAL is generally a superset of all other compatible implementations (to within nitpicks).\f2\par
\par
