\tab\tab The CONSOL\{E\} directive returns the contents of the front \tab\tab\tab panel switches to allow storage of the value into an \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly-time symbol:\par
\par
\tab\tab SW=\tab CONSOL\tab\tab /STORE CONSOLE SWITCHES IN SW.\par
\par
\tab\tab Conditional assembly can use the front panel switch register \tab\tab value for a variety of purposes as determined by the \tab\tab\tab\tab programmer.\par
\par
DATE\par
\par
\tab\tab The DATE directive returns the low-order 12-bits of the \tab\tab\tab current system date to allow storage of the value into an \tab\tab\tab assembly-time symbol:\par
\par
\tab\tab CURDAY=   DATE\tab\tab /STORE THE DATE IN CURDAY.\par
\par
\tab\tab Conditional assembly can use the date for a variety of \tab\tab\tab purposes including creating a current version of the \tab\tab\tab\tab program.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Future releases of P?S/8 PAL will likely include the \tab\tab\tab\i DATEHI\i0  directive to obtain the high-order six bits of the \tab\tab\tab full current system date.  The two values can be combined to \tab\tab form the complete date (in the range of 01-JAN-1900 through \tab\tab 31-DEC-2411).\par
\par
DECIMA\{L\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The internal radix for mathematical expressions is set to \tab\tab\tab DECIMA\{L\}; the default radix is OCTAL.\par
\par
DEVICE\par
\par
\tab\tab The DEVICE directive takes a four-character string argument. \tab\tab It is used to form the device-name string used to reference \tab\tab P?S/8 non-system device handlers and OS/8 device handlers:\par
\par
\tab\tab DEVICE    DTA1\tab\tab /REFERENCE TO DTA1:\par
\par
\tab\tab The DEVICE directive creates two 12-bit words containing the \tab\tab packed six-bit ASCII text characters of the argument.  In \tab\tab\tab the example given, the output would consist of 0424 followed \tab\tab by 0161.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The DEVICE directive is not affected by the setting of \tab\tab the /J option switch.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
DTORG \i expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The DTORG directive is intended to provide target storage \tab\tab\tab block number information when generating operating system \tab\tab\tab components in systems such as Typeset-8.  See Notes 1, 2 and \tab\tab 3.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \i expression\i0  is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned integer to \tab\tab designate a target storage block on a device such as \tab\tab\tab\tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The implementation of the DTORG is extremely \tab\tab\tab\tab problematic; see sections 2.61, 2.62 and 2.63 of this\par
\tab\tab document for more information.\par
\par
EJECT \i optional-string-value\cf3\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab\cf0 The EJECT directive causes assembler listing output to \tab\tab\tab advance to the next page.\par
\par
\tab\tab If used with the \i optional-string-value\i0 , the title on the \tab\tab\tab next page is set to the \i optional-string-value\i0  text (subject \tab\tab to truncation).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Statements containing the EJECT directive are not \tab\tab\tab shown in the assembler listing output.\par
\par
ENBITS\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENBITS directive is used in PAL10 to enable the bitmap\par
\tab\tab of the latest memory field when either a new FIELD\par
 \tab\tab directive is used or the end of the assembly.  This feature \tab\tab is not implemented in any other PAL assembler; the ENBITS \tab\tab\tab directive is provided for compatibility purposes only.  See \tab\tab Note 2.\par
\par
ENDBIN\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive closes the current binary file.  \tab\tab\tab\tab Additional binary output (if any) will be stored in the next \tab\tab binary output file.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive is ignored if binary output generation \tab\tab is not enabled in the overall assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab The ENDBIN directive is useful when creating optional \tab\tab\tab\tab program segments from a master assembly file to be loaded\tab\tab  \tab separately.  It is supported primarily for compatibility \tab\tab\tab with the paper-tape system assembler PAL III.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
ENPUNC\{H\}\par
\par
\tab\tab Binary output generation may have been turned off; the \tab\tab\tab ENPUNC\{H\} directive (re-)enables binary output generation.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The ENPUNC\{H\} directive is ignored if binary output \tab\tab\tab\tab generation is not enabled in the overall assembly.  See Note \tab\tab 4.\par
\par
ERROR \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The ERROR directive causes a deliberate error and error \tab\tab\tab message which will prevent automatic chaining to P?S/8 BIN.  \par
\par
\tab\tab Well developed PDP-8 programs may contain safeguards to \tab\tab\tab prevent undesirable scenarios such as having a definition \tab\tab\tab fall out of a required narrow range of addresses (or in some \tab\tab cases a unique required value).  This will help future \tab\tab\tab program maintainers from making logical blunders.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The value of the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  will be \tab\tab\tab\tab displayed as part of the error message to help pinpoint the \tab\tab problem such as in the example that follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab *7\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /APPROPRIATE STARTING LOCATION.\par
\par
LOCTM1,\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /TEMPORARY STORAGE.\par
LOCTM2,\tab .-.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab /ANOTHER TEMPORARY (WRONG PLACE!)\par
\par
\tab\tab AUTOXR=\tab .\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS WRONG! SHOULD BE 0010.\par
\par
\tab\tab MYPTR=    AUTOXR+7\tab\tab /USED AS AN AUTO-INDEX REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO    MYPTR&7770-10\tab <ERROR MYPTR>\par
\par
\tab\tab The value of MYPTR is 0020 instead of staying within the \tab\tab\tab required range of 0010 through 0017; as such, it is not \tab\tab\tab capable of being used as an auto-index register as the \tab\tab\tab program logic demands.  Hopefully, the error message\par
\tab\tab\highlight3 ER  0020  AT LOCTM2+0001 (0011)\highlight0  (where 0011 is the current \tab\tab\tab location counter of the assembly in this example), is \tab\tab\tab\tab sufficient to aid the maintenance programmer in correcting \tab\tab\tab the mistake.  (The LOCTM2 location is inappropriately placed \tab\tab in the auto-index register area ruining the program logic. \tab\tab\tab By moving LOCTM2 to another area of page-zero memory, the \tab\tab\tab problem is eliminated; program reassembly will not trigger \tab\tab\tab the error-detection safeguard.)\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is not used, the \tab\tab\tab error message will output \highlight3 ER  0000\highlight0 .\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\par
\cf0 EXPUNG\{E\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The EXPUNG\{E\} directive is used to clear the entire symbol \tab\tab\tab table at the start of an assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab All permanent symbols are removed (except directives).  The \tab\tab program must define all symbols used (including MRI symbols \tab\tab using the FIXMRI directive).\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
\tab\tab By deleting the internal symbol table, it is possible \tab\tab\tab\tab certain programs may be capable of assembly on small memory \tab\tab configurations.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The source code of P?S/8 PAL performs an EXPUNG\{E\} at \tab\tab the beginning of the assembly; P?S/8 PAL supports many \tab\tab\tab symbols not ordinarily found in many assemblers (such as IOT \tab\tab instructions for several peripherals and full PDP-8/E \tab\tab\tab\tab\ul\i Extended Arithmetic Element\ulnone\i0  [\i EAE\i0 ]).\par
\par
FIELD \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIELD directive is used to set a designated memory field \tab\tab for binary loading of statements that follow.  If the \tab\tab\tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is present, it is evaluated to \tab\tab\tab set the desired field; if there is no argument, the next \tab\tab\tab memory field is selected.\par
\par
\tab\tab When a FIELD directive is used, all open literal pools are \tab\tab\tab output and reset to default values.  If the /0 option switch \tab\tab is set, an automatic *200 will also be generated overriding \tab\tab the previous value of the current location counter.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Memory fields up to a maximum of 256K are supported \tab\tab\tab for compatibility with the KT8A option; however, all \tab\tab\tab\tab standard binary loaders can only load binary generated in \tab\tab\tab the normal 32K address space (field 0 through field 7).  \tab\tab\tab Future releases of P?S/8 may include a KT8A-specific loader \tab\tab that will require the optional hardware to operate.\par
\par
FILENA\{ME\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The FILENA\{ME\} directive is used to create a four word text \tab\tab string consisting of an OS/8 six-character file name and a \tab\tab\tab two-character file extension:\par
\par
\tab\tab FILENAME  FOOBAR.PA\tab /STATIC FILE NAME STRING.\par
\par
\tab\tab The FILENA\{ME\} directive creates four 12-bit words \tab\tab\tab\tab containing the packed six-bit ASCII text characters of the \tab\tab\tab argument.  In the example given, the output would consist of \tab\tab 0617, 1702, 0122 and 2001.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 supports the FILENA\{ME\} directive for compatibility \tab\tab\tab with PAL8.  Future P?S/8 systems will support the P?S/8\par
 \tab\tab SHELL with an extended assembler based on P?S/8 PAL to be \tab\tab\tab known as XPAL.  XPAL will support an option to extend the \tab\tab\tab FILENA\{ME\} directive to support SHELL directory file names.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The FILENA\{ME\} directive is not affected by the \tab\tab\tab\tab setting of the /J option switch.\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
FIXMRI\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIXMRI directive is used to modify a standard equate \tab\tab\tab statement so the assembler treats the symbol as a \ul\i Memory\ulnone\i0  \tab\tab\tab\ul\i Reference Instruction\ulnone\i0  (\i MRI\i0 ).  MRI symbols modify the \tab\tab\tab\tab expression evaluation so that the PDP-8 addressing rules are \tab\tab applied instead of simple \ul\i inclusive or\ulnone\i0  (.\i OR\i0 ) logic which is \tab\tab the default method of expression evaluation:\par
\par
\tab\tab FIXMRI    INC=\tab 2000\tab\tab\tab /ISZ PRESUMED TO NEVER SKIP.\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIXMRI directive is also used to define floating-point \tab\tab\tab pseudo-instructions that generally follow the MRI addressing \tab\tab rules as part of several software floating-point packages.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the EXPUNG\{E\} directive is used, FIXMRI is needed to \tab\tab\tab redefine the standard symbols that are MRI type (such as \tab\tab\tab\i TAD\i0 , \i DCA\i0 , etc.).\par
\par
FIXTAB\par
\par
\tab\tab The FIXTAB directive is used after certain symbols are \tab\tab\tab defined that the programmer desires to be treated as \tab\tab\tab\tab permanent symbols.  All such initialization should occur at \tab\tab the start of the assembly to establish the permanent symbol \tab\tab table (perhaps after the use of the EXPUNG\{E\} directive).\par
\par
\tab\tab All permanent symbols will not be included in the cross-\tab\tab\tab reference output (if the /X option switch is in effect) or \tab\tab\tab the symbol table printout at the end of the assembly (if the \tab\tab /S option switch is in effect) unless the /A option switch \tab\tab\tab is also set.\par
\par
I\par
\par
\tab\tab The I directive is used in all PDP-8 MRI statements \tab\tab\tab\tab involving indirect references.  This forces bit[3] to be set \tab\tab so the generated instruction conforms to the PDP-8 \tab\tab\tab\tab addressing rules (after the evaluated expression is adjusted \tab\tab according to the MRI addressing rules in effect) for \tab\tab\tab\tab indirect addressing.  See Note 5.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
IFDEF \i symbol-name\i0  \highlight3 <\highlight0\i statements\highlight3\i0 >\highlight0\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFDEF directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The symbol table is searched for the specified \i symbol-name\i0 .  \tab\tab If the symbol is present and defined, the \i statements\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are assembled.  If the symbol \tab\tab\tab is either not present or not defined at that point in the \tab\tab\tab assembly, the \i statements\i0  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are \tab\tab\tab ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \i statements\i0  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a \i nested\i0  manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The contents of comments are scanned for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when \tab\tab conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFDEF directive is often used to include other symbols \tab\tab\tab or data into an assembly if relevant:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFDEF     DECTAPE   <WC=\tab 7754>\tab /DEFINE WORD COUNT.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Unlike ordinary references to symbols, attempting to \tab\tab\tab locate an undefined symbol does not cause an error.\par
\par
IFNDEF \i symbol-name\i0  \highlight3 <\highlight0\i statements\highlight3\i0 >\highlight0\par
\par
  \tab\tab The IFNDEF directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The symbol table is searched for the specified \i symbol-name\i0 .  \tab\tab If the symbol not present, the \i statements\i0  contained within \tab\tab\tab the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are assembled.  If the symbol is present and \tab\tab\tab defined at that point in the assembly, the \i statements\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \i statements\i0  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a \i nested\i0  manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The contents of comments are scanned for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when \tab\tab conditional assembly is in effect.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
\tab\tab The IFNDEF directive is often used to initialize a symbol \tab\tab\tab default value:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF    NEWDATA   <NEWDATA=1>\tab /GET NEW DATA.\par
\par
\tab\tab In the example above, the conditional statement is assembled \tab\tab during pass one of the assembly.  During pass two the \tab\tab\tab\tab statement is ignored because the symbol is defined.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Unlike ordinary references to symbols, attempting to \tab\tab\tab locate an undefined symbol does not cause an error.\par
\par
IFNZRO \i expression\i0  \highlight3 <\highlight0\i statements\highlight3\i0 >\highlight0\par
\cf3\par
\cf0   \tab\tab The IFNZRO directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The specified \i expression\i0  is evaluated.  If the result is \tab\tab\tab non-zero, the \i statements\i0  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are \tab\tab\tab assembled.  If the result is zero, the \i statements\i0  contained \tab\tab within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \i statements\i0  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a \i nested\i0  manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The contents of comments are scanned for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when \tab\tab conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFNZRO directive is often used to check an assembly \tab\tab\tab parameter range:\par
\par
\tab\tab AUTOXR=   11\tab\tab\tab /THIS IS WRONG!  SHOULD BE 0010.\par
\par
\tab\tab MYPTR=    AUTOXR+7\tab\tab /USED AS AN AUTO-INDEX REGISTER.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO    MYPTR&7770-10\tab <ERROR MYPTR>\par
\par
\tab\tab In the example above, the range of MYPTR must be between \tab\tab\tab 0010 and 0017.  If this is not correct, an error message is \tab\tab issued.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
IFZERO \i expression\i0  \highlight3 <\highlight0\i statements\highlight3\i0 >\highlight0\par
\cf3\par
\cf0   \tab\tab The IFZERO directive is one of the conditional assembly \tab\tab\tab features of the PAL language.\par
\par
\tab\tab The specified \i expression\i0  is evaluated.  If the result is \tab\tab\tab zero, the \i statements\i0  contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are \tab\tab\tab\tab assembled.  If the result is non-zero, the \i statements\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab contained within the \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  are ignored.\par
\par
\tab\tab The \i statements\i0  can span an indefinite number of lines of \tab\tab\tab source code as necessary.  Conditionals are occasionally \tab\tab\tab used within other conditionals in a \i nested\i0  manner.  The \tab\tab\tab count of  \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  must be carefully maintained to achieve an \tab\tab error-free assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The contents of comments are scanned for \highlight3 <\highlight0  and \highlight3 >\highlight0  when \tab\tab conditional assembly is in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab The IFZERO directive is often used to create assembler \tab\tab\tab default values:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO    OURFLD   <\par
\par
\tab\tab BUFFER=   4000\tab\tab\tab /USE FIELD 0 VALUE.>\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO\tab OURFLD-1 <\par
\par
\tab\tab BUFFER=   6000\tab\tab\tab /USE FIELD 1 VALUE.>\par
\par
\tab\tab In the example above, a parameter must be set to a value \tab\tab\tab consistent with an overall assembly parameter that can have \tab\tab multiple values.\par
\par
LMODE\par
\par
\tab\tab The LMODE directive is provided in the dual mode assembly \tab\tab\tab module to enable LINC mode addressing and certain symbols \tab\tab\tab unique to LINC mode.  See Note 1.\par
\par
NOBITS\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOBITS directive is used in PAL10 to disable the\par
 \tab\tab bitmap of the latest memory field when either a new FIELD \tab\tab\tab directive is used or the end of the assembly.  This feature \tab\tab is not implemented in any other PAL assembler; the NOBITS \tab\tab\tab directive is provided for compatibility purposes only.  See \tab\tab Note 2.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
NOPUNC\{H\}\par
\par
\tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive turns off binary output generation\par
\tab\tab during the assembly without affecting the assembler's\par
\tab\tab current location counter.  Using the ENPUNC\{H\} directive\par
\tab\tab later in the assembly will restore binary output.\par
\par
  \tab\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} directive is ignored if binary output \tab\tab\tab\tab generation is not enabled in the overall assembly.  See Note \tab\tab 4.\par
\par
OCTAL\par
\par
\tab\tab The internal radix for mathematical expressions is set to \tab\tab\tab OCTAL.  While the default radix is OCTAL, an earlier section \tab\tab of the source file may have changed the radix to DECIMA\{L\}.  \tab\tab Any programming section where the current radix is in doubt \tab\tab should include a usage of the OCTAL directive.\par
\par
PAGE \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The PAGE directive is used to set an origin setting at the \tab\tab\tab lowest location on a PDP-8 memory page for binary loading of \tab\tab statements that follow.  If the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  \tab\tab is present, it is evaluated to set the desired page; if \tab\tab\tab there is no argument, the next memory page is selected.\par
\par
\tab\tab Selecting a new page will cause the output of the \tab\tab\tab\tab literal table of the previous page in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The default origin of any assembly is 0200; this is \tab\tab\tab equivalent to using the PAGE 1 directive.\par
\par
PAUSE \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The PAUSE directive has two different usages:\par
\par
\tab\tab When used without the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0 , the PAUSE \tab\tab directive indicates the rest of the latest input file will \tab\tab\tab be ignored.  This usage of the PAUSE directive is provided \tab\tab\tab for compatibility with the paper-tape operating system PAL \tab\tab\tab III assembler.\par
\par
\tab\tab When used with the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0 , a PAUSE \tab\tab\tab message is displayed during each pass of the assembly.  This \tab\tab is a user-defined function and is not an error message per \tab\tab\tab se (although the programmer can define values to specific \tab\tab\tab messages to describe some internal situation).\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\par
  \tab\tab When developing extremely long programs, programmers \tab\tab\tab\tab may use the PAUSE directive to signal the current program \tab\tab\tab section and/or the start (or end) of each assembly pass.\par
\tab\tab For more information see Section 2.03 of this document.\par
\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1 PMODE\par
\par
\tab\tab The PMODE directive is provided in the dual mode assembly \tab\tab\tab module to enable PDP-8 mode addressing and certain symbols \tab\tab\tab unique to PDP-8 mode.  See Note 1.\par
\par
PQS\par
\par
\tab\tab The PQS directive is a unique feature of P?S/8 PAL.  It can \tab\tab be used in conditional assembly statements to allow special-\tab\tab purpose programming only supported by P?S/8 PAL; alternate \tab\tab\tab statements can attempt to provide compatibility with other \tab\tab\tab assemblers.  Unfortunately, certain powerful features of\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL are not available in other assemblers; crude \tab\tab\tab alternatives have been used to provide a functional, albeit \tab\tab inferior, result.\par
\par
\tab\tab Features of P?S/8 PAL such as conditional literals have been \tab\tab instrumental in implementing several very large programs; \tab\tab\tab conditional assembly has been used to provide inferior \tab\tab\tab support of statically-determined page zero literals through \tab\tab the use of manual optimization at some point in the \tab\tab\tab\tab development.  However, as additional development is \tab\tab\tab\tab performed, the static manual optimization tends to drift \tab\tab\tab while assembly with P?S/8 is always current.  Only \tab\tab\tab\tab additional manual optimization can sync up the relevant \tab\tab\tab changes.  As such, the PQS directive has been used \tab\tab\tab\tab extensively to allow PAL8 to replicate the latest\par
 \tab\tab static optimization until further development with P?S/8 can \tab\tab be performed.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL will include a more \tab\tab\tab\tab advanced assembler partially based on P?S/8 PAL tentatively \tab\tab to be known as XPAL.  P?S/8 SHELL XPAL will also support the \tab\tab PQS directive; however, since XPAL will be a more \tab\tab\tab\tab comprehensive assembler, a companion \i PQSX\i0  directive will be \tab\tab implemented.  This will allow programmers to take advantage \tab\tab of the advanced features of both assemblers.\par
\par
RELOC \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The RELOC directive performs much of the functionality of \tab\tab\tab the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives in a more compact and \tab\tab elegant form.  The \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is evaluated \tab\tab to form a relocation factor that sets up an offset between \tab\tab\tab the assembler's current location counter and the binary \tab\tab\tab output origin.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Note: Since the relocation factor is applied to all binary\par
\tab\tab output, the RELOC directive can be used in conjunction with \tab\tab literals.\par
\par
\tab\tab Additional usage of the RELOC directive with the\par
\i\tab\tab optional-numeric-expression\i0  causes cumulative relocation, \tab\tab\tab which can be useful in certain obscure situations.  The \tab\tab\tab RELOC directive used without the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  \tab\tab resets the relocation factor to zero.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: While PAL8 is the only other assembler that supports\par
\tab\tab the RELOC directive, there are certain differences:\par
\par
\tab\tab a)\tab PAL8 flags statements detected as \i apparently\i0\tab  relocated\par
\tab\tab with \highlight3 *\highlight0 .  P?S/8 PAL flags statements in a similar manner, but\par
\tab\tab only if relocation is actually in effect.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab If a statement of the form\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab RELOC .\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab is used in the source program, relocation is reset to \tab\tab\tab\tab zero; however, PAL8 will only cancel the statement\par
\tab\tab\tab relocation flag when the RELOC directive is used\par
\tab\tab\tab without the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0 .\par
\par
\tab\tab b)\tab PAL8 does not flag statements that are relocated \tab by\par
\tab\tab\tab the use of the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL outputs the \highlight3 *\highlight0  on all statements actually \tab\tab\tab\tab relocated regardless of method of relocation.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Certain advanced programming methods include the \tab\tab\tab simultaneous usage of the NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives \tab\tab and the RELOC directive; this is necessary when there are \tab\tab\tab several independent aspects of relocation in affect at the \tab\tab\tab same time.\par
\par
SEGMNT \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The SEGMNT directive is used to set the field and origin \tab\tab\tab setting to the quarter memory field segment specified by the \tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  in the range of 00 (field 0, \tab\tab\tab location 0000) through 37 (field 7,location 6000) as \tab\tab\tab\tab required by the LINC architecture of the LINC-8 and PDP-12.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is present, it is \tab\tab\tab\tab evaluated to set the desired segment; if there is no \tab\tab\tab\tab argument, the next memory segment is selected\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf0\par
\tab\tab Note: Segment 2 is the default value for LINC mode assembly \tab\tab followed by an origin of 0020 (04020).  This is equivalent \tab\tab\tab to SEGMNT 2 followed by *20 (in LINC mode, addresses are \tab\tab\tab specified within a segment).  See Note 1.\par
\par
SIXBIT \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The SIXBIT directive creates 12-bit words packed with six-\tab\tab\tab bit characters taken from the \i text-string\i0  (other than \tab\tab\tab\tab delimiter characters).  The encoding is six-bit ASCII text \tab\tab\tab as used with similar directives in assemblers for DEC 18-bit \tab\tab and 36-bit systems (which is not the PDP-8 standard).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the number of characters in the \i text-string\i0  is \tab\tab\tab even, an additional 12-bit word of 0000 is created as a \tab\tab\tab delimiter unless the /J option switch is set.  For more \tab\tab\tab information see Section 2.41 of this document.\par
\par
SKIP \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The SKIP directive is used to create deliberately blank \tab\tab\tab lines in the listing output of an assembly.  The\par
\tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is evaluated as an unsigned \tab\tab\tab 12-bit integer.  Blank lines are created as many times as \tab\tab\tab necessary unless the value is zero, in which case no blank \tab\tab\tab lines are created.  If the \i optional-numeric-expression\i0  is \tab\tab\tab not present, one blank line will be created.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: All lines containing the SKIP directive will not be \tab\tab\tab displayed in the listing.\par
\par
TEXT \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The TEXT directive creates 12-bit words packed with six-\tab\tab\tab bit characters taken from the \i text-string\i0  (other than \tab\tab\tab\tab delimiter characters).  The encoding is six-bit ASCII text \tab\tab\tab as typically used on the PDP-8 (which is not compatible with \tab\tab similar directives in assemblers for DEC 18-bit and 36-bit \tab\tab\tab systems).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the number of characters in the \i text-string\i0  is \tab\tab\tab even, an additional 12-bit word of 0000 is created as a \tab\tab\tab delimiter unless the /J option switch is set.  For more \tab\tab\tab information see Section 2.41 of this document.\par
\par
TITLE \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The TITLE directive is used to set the title field of the \tab\tab\tab page header used on every listing page if the /L and /N \tab\tab\tab option switches are set.  The \i text-string\i0  will be used \tab\tab\tab (subject to truncation) starting with the next printed page.\page\cf2 Appendix B (continued)\cf3\f0\par
\cf0\f1\par
\tab\tab Normally, the title field is taken from the first line of \tab\tab\tab the first input file; the TITLE directive can only affect \tab\tab\tab subsequent output.  However, the TITLE directive can be used \tab\tab to override the first page title field by using the XLIST \tab\tab\tab directive beforehand:\par
\par
\tab\tab /\tab EXAMPLE WHERE THIS IS NOT USED IN THE TITLE FIELD.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab XLIST\tab\tab\tab\tab /TURN OFF LISTING NOW.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab TITLE\tab MY TITLE\tab\tab /USE DESIRED TITLE INSTEAD.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab XLIST\tab\tab\tab\tab /TURN LISTING ON NOW.\par
\par
XLIST \i optional-numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The XLIST directive is used to hide portions of a listing \tab\tab\tab file (assuming the /L option switch is set).  If the \tab\tab\tab\tab\i optional-numeric-expression\i0  evaluates to a non-zero value, \tab\tab\tab the listing is turned off.  If it evaluates to zero, the \tab\tab\tab listing output is restored.  If no argument is given, the \tab\tab\tab listing output state is reversed from its former state.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: All lines containing the XLIST directive are hidden.\par
\par
Z\par
\par
\tab\tab The Z directive is provided for compatibility with the PAL \tab\tab\tab II assembler of the paper-tape operating system.  All PDP-8 \tab\tab assemblers starting with PAL III are capable of properly \tab\tab\tab setting arguments to MRI type instructions according to the \tab\tab rules of the PDP-8 architecture.  PAL II only implements a \tab\tab\tab subset where all symbols are erroneously placed on the \tab\tab\tab current page.  When necessary, the Z directive is used on \tab\tab\tab all statements that actually address page zero to remove the \tab\tab current page bit from the generated value.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL III was originally written in PAL II.  Later \tab\tab\tab releases of the source code were modified to require a \tab\tab\tab binary copy of PAL III to assemble its own source code.\par
\par
\tab\tab It is known that PAL8 ignores the Z directive while P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab PAL implements the original intention.\par
\par
ZBLOCK \i numeric-expression\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The ZBLOCK directive is used to create blocks of data set to \tab\tab 0000.  The \i numeric-expression\i0  is evaluated as an unsigned \tab\tab\tab integer; the value determines how many words are generated.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the \i numeric-expression\i0  evaluates to zero, no words \tab\tab are generated.\page\cf3 Appendix B Notes\par
\cf0\par
1)\tab Certain directives are only available if LINC mode dual assembly \tab is enabled using either the /8 or /9 option switches.  They are \tab provided primarily for compatibility with the DIAL-MS dual mode \tab assembler.  Due to the nature of LINC mode expressions that use \tab one's complement arithmetic, the programmer must be aware of \tab the attendant quirks (such as positive and negative zero values).\par
\par
2)\tab Despite not being particularly associated with dual mode \tab assembly, certain directives are provided only if either the /8 \tab or /9 command-line option switches are set.  These directives \tab will be moved to the general symbol table load in a future \tab release to avoid this erroneous dependency; for the present, dual \tab mode assembly must be enabled.  Additional directives not \tab currently supported may be added during the program upgrade.\par
\par
3)\tab The DTORG directive should not be used at this time as its \tab presence in the source code causes the binary output to be \tab corrupted.  See Sections 2.61, 2.62 and 2.63 of this document for\par
\tab more information.\par
\par
4)\tab The NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives are often used to relocate \tab binary output as part of an overlay structure.  The assembler \tab current location counter can be updated while the binary output \tab origin remains fixed.  The program segment loads into an address \tab space that can be moved or stored elsewhere while properly \tab assembled for its ultimate execution address space:\par
\par
\tab\tab *6000\tab\tab\tab /WHERE THE GENERATED CODE LOADS.\par
\par
\tab\tab NOPUNCH\tab\tab\tab /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.\par
\par
\tab\tab *7600\tab\tab\tab /WHERE THE CODE EXECUTES.\par
\par
\tab\tab ENPUNCH\tab\tab\tab /UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.\par
\par
\tab Note: The NOPUNC\{H\} and ENPUNC\{H\} directives are traditional \tab features of several PDP-8 assemblers including PAL10; literals\par
\tab cannot be used with this form of relocation mechanism.\par
\par
\tab The RELOC directive can accomplish a similar form of code \tab relocation including literals if necessary.  However, it is only \tab supported by P?S/8 PAL and PAL8.\page\cf2 Appendix B Notes (continued)\cf0\par
\par
5)\tab The I directive changes meaning if LINC mode assembly is in \tab effect.  Since there are no indirect references in LINC \tab programming, the I directive is redefined to force bit[7] on.  In \tab the context of LINC addressing, the I directive means either \tab immediate mode (when used with instructions where the operand \tab immediately follows the instruction) or auto-indexed mode (when \tab used with references to locations 0001-0017).\par
\par
\tab Many LINC instructions take immediate arguments:\par
\par
\tab\tab LMODE\tab\tab\tab /LINC MODE ASSEMBLY.\par
\par
\tab\tab LDA I;    3000\tab\tab /LOAD THE AC WITH 3000.\par
\tab\tab ADA  ;    FOO \tab\tab /ADD THE CONTENTS OF FOO\par
\par
\tab The first instruction above uses the I bit to indicate the \tab argument (3000) is immediately after the instruction.  The second \tab instruction does not use the I bit; as such, the address of the \tab operand follows (which ironically means it is an indirect \tab reference to the actual operand).\par
\par
\tab The PDP-8 references to locations 0010-0017 are auto-incremented \tab when referenced indirectly.  In LINC mode, references to 0001-\tab 0017 are ordinary indirect references unless the I directive is \tab used:\par
\par
\tab\tab LMODE\tab\tab\tab /LINC MODE ASSEMBLY.\par
\par
\tab\tab ADA    17\tab\tab\tab /ADD WHAT IS POINTED TO.\par
\tab\tab ADA I  17\tab\tab\tab /ADD NEXT VALUE AS WELL.\par
\par
\tab The first instruction uses the present contents of 0017 to point \tab to the operand.  The second instruction first increments the \tab contents of 0017 to point to the (next) operand.\par
\par
\tab Certain LINC instructions have special meaning for references to \tab location 0000; these instructions have specific addressing needs \tab which do not involve the I bit.  The auto-increment references to \tab locations 0001-0017 clearly are the inspiration for the PDP-8 \tab auto-increment usage of locations 0010-0017.  The range was \tab shortened to allow PDP-8 interrupt handling at the lower \tab addresses.  (The LINC was designed without an interrupt \tab structure; however, some unofficial add-ons were known to exist. \tab The LINC-8 does not support LINC interrupts.  The PDP-12 defines \tab a LINC mode interrupt analogous to the PDP-8 interrupt but to an \tab alternate location.)\page\cf2 Appendix B Notes (continued)\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The PDP-8 cannot use references to 0010-0017 without auto-\tab increment; to this limited extent, the LINC instruction set\par
\tab is superior to the PDP-8 instruction set.  LINC mode addressing \tab does not allow indirect usage of any other locations (other than \tab pointer locations immediately after  the instructions).  There is \tab no concept of a page zero in the LINC architecture, and there are \tab only three instructions that address all of memory directly. \tab However, \tab all of memory in this case is limited to 1024 \tab locations, not the \tab PDP-8 norm of 4096 locations.  There is no \tab concept of a subroutine call mechanism; the JMP instruction \tab attempts to act as \tab a stand-in.  However, if JMP instructions are \tab used in the subroutine, various fixups (read \i kludges\i0  here) are \tab needed to protect the return address.  All features considered, \tab system architecture experts are welcome to weigh in with their \tab own opinions which may greatly differ with the author's \tab viewpoint.  (Few operating systems for the LINC architecture \tab support PDP-8 programming; all known implementations are quite \tab inferior to the PDP-8 assemblers mentioned elsewhere in this \tab document.  To the author's knowledge, none of them support mixed \tab dual assembly to any extent; rather, PDP-8 programming and binary \tab loading support is considered an independent concept.  This is \tab especially disappointing for use on the PDP-12 where mixed dual \tab mode assembly is quite common due to the nature of the \tab architecture.)\par
\par
End of Appendix B\page\f0\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f1\par
Appendix C - P?S/8 PAL conditional literals and related topics\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL supports an extension beyond the literal features \tab common to other assembler as \tab described earlier in this document, \tab which is known as the \i conditional\i0  literal (or occasionally as the \tab\i dependent\i0  literal since the code generated is dependent on \tab specific conditions in effect throughout the assembly).\par
\par
  \tab While conditional literals are an important \tab and powerful feature \tab of practical PDP-8 assembly language programming, conditional \tab literal support is unique to P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
  \tab Note: Future directions \tab of P?S/8 support include the P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  \tab overlay which will include a new \tab assembler program partially\par
\tab based on P?S/8 PAL; conditional literal support will be\par
\tab consistent with P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
Alternate syntax considerations of conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab To aid in transitioning from other assemblers (such as Macro-11) \tab to PDP-8 programming, the conditional literal uses syntax similar \tab to that of certain other assemblers:\par
\par
\f0\tab\tab TAD\tab\tab #10\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0010 FROM SOMEWHERE.\par
\par
\f1\tab While addressing rules for the PDP-8 differ from other computer\tab architectures, literals expressed this way are a familiar concept \tab to many programmers accustomed to the software environment of \tab these other systems; by using conditional literals, P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab will help programmers make the transition to the PDP-8 assembly\par
\tab environment easier.  See Note 1.\par
\par
Conditional literal syntax and addressing rules.\par
\par
\tab Conditional literals take the form of the following:\par
\par
\f0\tab\tab TAD\tab\tab #3\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0003 FROM CHOSEN PAGE.\par
\f1\par
\tab In this example, the assembler will make a dynamic decision as to \tab which type of literal to use:\par
\par
\tab a)  If the program already contains a matching Page Zero\par
\tab     literal due to other program statements using the identical\par
\tab     value, this statement will use that existing Page Zero \tab\tab\tab     literal address.\par
\par
\tab b)  Assuming a) fails, If the program already contains a matching \tab     current page literal value due to other program statements,\par
\tab     the statement will use that current page literal address.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued).\par
\cf0\par
\tab c)  Assuming both a) and b) fail, the assembler will create a new \tab     current page literal and use that current page literal\par
\tab     address.  It is possible other statements on the current page \tab     could benefit from this as in the case of current page\par
\tab     literals.\par
\par
\tab Unlike Page Zero and current page literals, there is no matching \tab optional trailing character.  As such, the seldom-needed feature \tab of nested expression elements is not available when using \tab conditional literals.  See Note 1.\par
\par
\tab In certain cases, it will be necessary to calculate the \tab particulars of an expression involved with any form of literals \tab in several equate statements.  In the case of the conditional \tab literal this is unavoidable.  See Notes 2 and 3.\par
\par
Source libraries and conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab Experienced programmers tend to create a series of commonly used \tab routines as starting points for some new programming \tab project.  \tab\par
\tab Unlike other architectures, (some constrained by ROM-\tab based \tab basic routines), the PDP-8 imposes no particular structure \tab whatsoever, other than a general convention that the last page of \tab field 0 (07600-07777) is reserved for whatever mechanism is used \tab to load the program.\par
\par
\tab Note: Depending on the particular operating system and certain \par
\tab specific configuration considerations, there may be additional \tab limitations imposed; however, the emphasis is always on minimal \tab intrusion regarding memory reservation.  P?S/8 contains specific\par
\tab information within the kernel memory in 07600-07777 to allow any\par
\tab conforming program to determine what, if any, additional\par
\tab limitations are in effect; other operating systems may implement\par
\tab alternate constraints on memory usage.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 adds basic read/write routines to designated storage \tab device(s) that are available by the nature of the P?S/8 design.\par
\tab By proper adherence to system guidelines, programs can be made \tab compatible with all supported machine models including the \tab DECmate series (which is substantially incompatible with all \tab previous 12-bit DEC computers).\par
\par
\tab As stated above, by conforming to P?S/8 system guidelines, any\par
\tab program can run under P?S/8 on any supported model; however,\par
\tab there is no hard and fast rule that this is required.  Any\par
\tab program can be written for any specific supported model or models \tab constrained only by the \tab designated hardware (and the minimal \tab memory reservation of P?S/8 itself, which must be observed to\par
\tab allow P?S/8 to load the executable form of the program.)\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued).\par
\cf0\par
\tab Since the foundational programming needed to support many of the \tab elements mentioned above will tend to be needed each \tab time (for \tab the most part, and not necessarily in the entirety in the case \tab of some elaborate packages), it is clearly advantageous to \tab develop working modules that can be organized into source \tab libraries for \tab each class of \tab functions desired.  See Note 4.\par
\par
\cf2\tab\cf0 Well-formed source library routines for use in the P?S/8 \tab environment should exclusively use conditional literals; wherever \tab possible, existing Page Zero \tab literals defined in the main \tab program will be used to determine \tab the specific \tab binary code\par
\tab generated.  As such, the code size of the library routines will \tab tend to be shorter without expending the effort to determine this \par
\tab manually \tab (as would be the case when using \tab other assemblers\par
\tab lacking this feature unique to P?S/8 PAL).  See Note 5.\par
\par
Problematic interaction with incompatible assemblers regarding conditional literals.\par
\par
\tab Attempts to maintain compatibility with other assemblers (such as \tab PAL8) can be quite frustrating when a programming project clearly \tab needs the conditional literal feature set to avoid excessive \tab development time.  Using P?S/8 PAL (and conditional literals), a \tab nearly finished project can produce an assembly listing which \tab must then be studied to highlight instances that were \tab successfully assembled using existing Page Zero literals.  This \tab would be the best starting point to create compatible source \tab code; however, any further changes to the source code can easily \tab invalidate the inter-symbol relationships that led to the \tab optimization in place.\par
\par
\tab Any work performed at this point leads only to a tenuous form of \tab compatibility; changes in the actual coding may not translate \tab into an efficient program, just merely a program that had been \tab efficient at the point in time chosen to implement the necessary \tab compatibility statements.  The result is hardly a good outcome, \tab but is the only way to achieve some measure of compatibility at \tab the expense of efficient design, achieved with minimal effort to \tab this point, then modified with additional effort that only \tab supports the tentative compatibility.\par
\par
Implementation of compatible coding.\tab\par
\par
\tab Conditional assembly techniques can be used to determine which \tab assembler is being used to process the source code and choose \tab alternate source code statements where necessary.  While this \tab tedious process produced the \tab same binary at the time the \tab effort is spent determining which statements to dual-code, the \tab alternate statements do \tab not dynamically adjust to the changing \tab requirements of an ongoing project (or perhaps due to \tab modifications made in the far future).\par
\par
\tab This is not by any means a completely viable alternative and \tab should only be attempted very close to the end of any project.  \tab Those responsible for future modification need to be advised of \tab potential consequences of changes made.  (This is why programmers \tab familiar with P?S/8 PAL abandon PAL8 for some portion of their \tab work.)\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab The following example illustrates how to write code that \tab assembles in a custom manner with P?S/8 PAL:\par
\f0\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab PQS\tab <PQS=0> \tab /THIS IS IGNORED BY P?S/8 PAL.\f1\par
\par
\tab\tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR THE OTHERS.>\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR P?S/8 PAL ONLY.>\par
\par
\tab Various techniques can be implemented for each instance of a \tab known conditional literal; the goal is to produce binary code \tab that is identical regardless of which assembler was used (unless \tab and until the overall source code is changed).  The following \tab serves as a minimal guideline for constructing compatible dual \tab statement sections:\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR P?S/8 PAL ONLY.\par
\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\tab\tab /ASSEMBLE FOR THE OTHERS.\par
\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab [77]\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab >\par
\par
\tab All statements similar to the above, where the conditional \tab literal successfully used Page Zero to form the code, must \tab consistently be expanded as in the example.\par
\par
\tab Additional techniques, such as surrounding the conditionalized\par
\tab code with judiciously chosen XLIST directive statements can\par
\tab achieve what appears to be unaffected code if desired:\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab\tab\tab /GENERALLY DON'T WANT TO SEE THIS.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab OFF\tab <OFF= 1>  /THIS TURNS LISTING OFF.\par
\par
\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab ON\tab <ON=\tab 0>\tab /THIS TURNS LISTING ON.\par
\par
\tab\tab XLIST\tab ON\tab\tab\tab /WHAT FOLLOWS WILL BE SEEN.\par
\par
\tab These parameters are used to debug the conditional sections.  If \tab unexpected output occurs, the definition of OFF can be \tab temporarily changed to reveal all conditional sections (at the \tab expense of paging of the code in a listing file) to aid in \tab determining what was miscoded, etc.  (Note that \i OFF\i0  and \i ON\i0  are \tab unimportant during pass 1 of the assembly as long as they are \tab defined before the end of the pass.)\page\tab\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab All dual-coded sections should start with XLIST OFF.  Each \tab section starts with an XLIST ON and ends with XLIST OFF.  \tab Following the entire conditional section XLIST ON should be used.  \tab (All statement lines with an instance of XLIST are not listed. \tab However, P?S/8 PAL listings will reveal gaps in the statement \tab numbers.  CREF sequences listing files incrementally and has no\par
\tab ability to detect the consequences of XLIST statements.)\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab\tab\tab /START OF CONDITIONAL SECTION.\par
\par
     \tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab >\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab PQS\tab <\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\par
     \tab AND\tab\tab [77]\tab\tab\tab /JUST SIX BITS.\par
     \tab XLIST\tab OFF\tab >\par
\par
     \tab XLIST\tab ON\tab\tab\tab /END OF THIS CONDITIONAL SECTION.\par
\par
\tab By using all of this overhead (repeated for each instance of a \tab conditional literal succeeding to use existing Page Zero \tab literals), what appears to be code devoid of conditional literals \tab is achieved (when assembled by PAL8/CREF); however, this is \tab achieved by the additional effort (which introduces obfuscation\par
\tab into the source code).\par
\par
More advanced techniques.\par
\par
\tab If the programmer chooses to spend additional time regarding this \tab set of issues, statements can be formed to trace conditional \tab literal statements that succeed in producing Page Zero usage.\par
\par
  \tab The following statement is to be used only once early in the \tab source code before any conditional literal considerations:\par
\par
\tab\tab ZCNT=\tab 0\tab\tab\tab /INITIALIZE SUCCESS COUNT.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Within each conditional section being traced, additional \tab statements are added just after the code statement using the \tab conditional literal:\par
\par
     \tab IFNZRO\tab TRC\tab <\par
\par
     \tab ZUSED=\tab #77\tab\tab\tab /WILL BE ADDRESS THAT WAS USED.\par
\par
     \tab IFZERO\tab ZUSED&200\tab <\par
\par
     \tab ZCNT=\tab ZCNT+1\tab\tab /COUNT THIS AS A SUCCESS.\par
\par
     \tab PAUSE\tab ZCNT\tab\tab\tab /OUTPUT CURRENT COUNT.\par
\par
     \tab PAUSE\tab .\tab\tab\tab /SHOW WHERE WE ARE\par
\par
          \tab\tab\tab\tab >\par
\par
     \tab\tab\tab\tab >      /END OF TRACE FOR THIS SECTION.\par
\par
\tab This additional coding is intended for all conditional literal \tab statements without regard to the likelihood of Page Zero usage; \tab the tracing statements are designed to reveal the statements that \tab succeeded so the programmer can monitor the effect of conditional \tab literal usage throughout the entire source file.\par
\par
\tab The PAUSE directive is a feature exclusive to P?S/8 PAL.  \tab Each \tab usage of the PAUSE directive outputs a message to the \tab prevailing \tab listing output device; the printout includes the four digit \tab octal evaluation of the arithmetic expression following the PAUSE \tab directive as well as an introductory message.  As used in the \tab example, the count of successful cases \tab is seen to grow with each \tab success; the address within the assembly is also shown to aid in \tab maintaining the changes required \tab as illustrated above.  (PAUSE \tab output is not an error message.)\par
\par
A real-world example of a large programming project that was affected by the use of conditional literals and backward \tab compatibility.\par
\par
\tab A very large PDP-8/E-based system was created for a corporate \tab user to be run on several \i live\i0  systems (and backup computers \tab which were \i offline\i0 ).  The project is one of the most ambitious \tab programs ever undertaken for the 32K PDP-8 environment.  It was \tab written as a standalone operating system with its own system \tab generation/creation routines for a pair of RK05 drives on an RK8E \tab with optional third-party serial-line multiplexors.  This system \tab was capable of running as many as 40 tasks in a cooperative \tab multitasking environment.\page\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Due to the sheer complexity (which included overlays of certain \tab maintenance and data recovery tools) this was perhaps the best \tab known example of productivity improvement achieved by the use \tab of conditional literals.  Had the feature not been used, \tab development time would have been radically increased.\par
\par
\tab However, an unfortunate postscript to this otherwise successful \tab example includes the following:\par
\par
\tab The small company division that contracted for this system was \tab also occasionally "managed" by individuals from a parent company \tab not only completely unfamiliar with the local operation, but also \tab totally ignorant of all things PDP-8.  As a result of their \tab actions, the company paid for many additional weeks of needless \tab makework to implement PAL8 compatibility as described above.\par
\par
  \tab They were warned at the time that future modifications would \tab likely result in increased development costs and time.  This is \tab exactly what happened during the course of several years as the \tab requirements gradually changed.  All of the necessary \tab conditionals were consistently implemented as described above, \tab and the costs \tab included the time spent reconciling the \tab predictable effects of the needless changes to the source code.\par
\par
\tab Since the entirety of this large application is completely \tab standalone by nature, these decisions were quite foolhardily.\par
\par
\tab It is interesting to note the parent company was eventually \tab forced to sell the local division to another large company, and \tab in fact, eventually was forced into bankruptcy.  The new owners \tab had \tab notions of their own which did not include the use of any \tab DEC products; they decided to dismantle the equipment and then \tab specified a replacement system that would cost several million \tab dollars.  However, since they also seriously devalued the entire \tab local company operation, they were forced to sell it to an even \tab larger company that eventually replaced everything with \tab overpriced PC \tab compatibles that could \tab have easily been produced \tab for far less overall cost.\page\tab\cf2 Appendix C (continued)\cf0\par
\par
\tab As of the writing of this document, the corporate world perceives\tab this operation as requiring tens of millions of dollars worth of \tab hardware despite company growth of less than 50% in the over \tab twenty years of operation the author of this document is familiar \tab with; this era ended with the head of the local operation seeking \tab early retirement due to all of the incompetent interference over \tab the years.\par
\par
Alternative methods to achieve a measure of compatibility.\par
\par
\tab Clearly, the use of conditional literals is a very useful \tab technique for most PDP-8 programming.  If there is a need to \tab produce a result that can be used in OS/8, there are appropriate \tab techniques available to allow P?S/8 PAL to be used for the \tab development.\par
\par
\tab a)  P?S/8 generally runs on any configuration that OS/8 can\par
\tab     support (including configurations OS/8 cannot run on due to \tab\tab     its design limitations regarding system device handler\par
 \tab     design).\par
\par
\tab b)  P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  is a bidirectional text file conversion program \tab     designed to facilitate moving PAL (and other language) source \tab     files between the two systems (in either direction of\par
\tab     conversion).  While each configuration is different, it is\par
\tab     generally possible to create a compatible transfer mechanism \tab     on most (if not all) of the devices supported by both\par
\tab     systems.\par
\par
\tab     With minimal effort,it is possible to maintain files in OS/8 \tab     and also transfer copies of the files to P?S/8 to allow\par
\tab     processing with P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\tab c)  For reasons unrelated to OS/8 binary compatibility as\par
\tab     discussed here, a near-term future component of P?S/8 is\par
\tab     being developed known as \i BINCON\i0 .  BINCON will be capable of\par
\tab     converting OS/8 absolute binary files from/to P?S/8 \i Slurp\par
\i0\tab     format binary files.  This will allow P?S/8 source\par
 \tab     development to not only continue to be written conforming to\par
 \tab     the subset of PAL8 compatibility (where feasible), but will\par
 \tab     also allow a misimplemented feature of PAL8 (DTORG which was\par
\tab     originally implemented for the benefit of the Typeset-8\par
\tab     group, which corrupts the binary output for all other\par
 \tab     purposes) to become useful for P?S/8 development in the near\par
 \tab     future.\par
\par
Certain P?S/8 source programs are intended to be assembled and loaded in the OS/8 environment; this produces a stand-alone system generation routine for a portion of current P?S/8 development.  BINCON will allow changes to this method while also maintaining compatibility with the current method; P?S/8 PAL will produce proper P?S/8 binary files which can then be converted to OS/8 format.  Programmers can use analogous techniques to take advantage of conditional literals yet maintain source files (and converted binary files) in OS/8.\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes.\par
\par
\cf0 1)\tab The syntax of the conditional literal was proposed as introduced \tab by the \highlight3\\\highlight0  character.  As of P?S/8 PAL Version 8Q, the \highlight3 #\highlight0  character \tab is used.  A cosmetic advantage of the original proposal was to\par
\tab allow pseudo-symmetrical statements to be formed:\par
\par
\tab TAD     \\3/\tab\tab /ADD 0003 FROM DYNAMICALLY CHOSEN PAGE.\par
\par
\tab No actual symmetry exists; in the example above, the line ends \tab with a comment starting just after the end of the conditional \tab literal with the first \highlight3 /\highlight0  character; to the casual eye, the \tab comment starts with the second \highlight3 /\highlight0  character (in the usual place \tab further to the right).\par
\par
\tab Since this provides a cosmetic improvement (and both characters \tab are undefined in the rest of the relevant assemblers), it is \tab conceivable future P?S/8 PAL releases will equally support both \tab characters for conditional literals (unless an alternate feature \tab is proposed to use one of the characters independently).\par
\par
2)\tab OS/8 PAL8 has several bugs in its arithmetic expression handling \tab that render it incompatible with P?S/8 PAL and TOPS-10 PAL10,\par
\tab which largely implement most features compatibly; however, these \tab weaknesses of PAL8 are avoidable for most (but not all) PDP-8 \tab programming projects.  (The areas of weakness and \tab incompatibilities of other assemblers \tab is the primary focus of\par
\tab this section.)\par
\par
\tab In general, P?S/8 program development maintains compatibility \tab with the PAL8 implementation \tab as released with \i OS/278\i0  Version 2.  \tab Certain bugs exist in earlier versions of PAL8 which were never \tab fixed; however, these are generally esoteric in nature and for \tab the most part easily avoided.  In a few specific instances, the\par
\tab exact syntax in statements within P?S/8 source code was slightly\par
\tab compromised to maintain compatibility with PAL8; however, this is \par
\tab admittedly a relatively minor issue.  As described elsewhere in\par
\tab this document, both PAL10 and PAL8 lack a key conceptual feature\par
\tab that is crucial to realistic large-scale program development.\cf3\par
\cf0\tab\par
\tab A large PDP-8 \tab project was written attempting to overcome this \tab serious issue \tab using conditional assembly techniques to manage\par
\tab the incompatibility (with no ability to fully overcome the\par
\tab problem).  During development, many assembly listings were\par
\tab analyzed to edit conditional assembly statements to best serve\par
\tab the project.  Since the project managers demanded that OS/8 PAL8\par
\tab be used, this was a less than satisfying outcome wasting time and \tab effort.\par
\par
\tab Note: In hindsight, the programmers should have fought management\par
\tab more aggressively to prove their decisions were detrimental to\par
\tab the company's bottom line.\par
\par
\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\par
\par
\cf0 3)\tab The use of parenthetical expressions in the context of literals \tab is the intended design goal.  Unfortunately, OS/8 PAL8 is poorly \tab implemented and has many unexpected limitations.  It is generally \tab recommended that such expressions be avoided unless the only \tab assemblers used are either TOPS-10 PAL10 or P?S/8 PAL which \tab correctly and completely implement Page Zero and current page \tab literals regardless of complexity.\par
\par
\tab By design, conditional literals must be calculated using separate \tab equate statements where applicable; however, the use of \tab parenthetical expressions is an obscure feature of the PAL \tab language which is seldom employed.  Unfortunately, this led to \tab failure to test poor implementations such as PAL8; with \tab regard to arithmetic expressions in general (which includes \tab related blunders not specifically connected to literal \tab expressions, such as flawed unary minus issues unique to PAL8). \tab\par
\par
\tab Note: When few programmers use obscure features, bugs tend to go \tab unnoticed and are less likely to be fixed.\par
\par
4)\tab Due to the versatility of the PDP-8 instruction set (most \tab especially the \i JMS\i0  instruction) there are several ways to create \tab callable subroutines in various forms that, while similar in \tab function, are not necessarily identical.  At the whim of the \tab programmer, either certain \i pet\i0  variants can be placed into a \tab source library, or perhaps competing routines each with some \tab stated tradeoffs where one routine may be more suitable than \tab another in any given application; the choice of routine can be \tab made during the project development.\line\line\tab For example, a subroutine may be defined for the purpose of \tab passing a text string meant to be displayed on the system \tab console.  Every aspect of the mechanism of such a subroutine is \tab potentially subject to change.  A subroutine entry point may be\par
\tab located near the end of Page Zero intended to be addressed\par
\tab directly by each caller.  Alternatively, a pointer may be placed \tab on Page Zero to allow callers scattered throughout memory equal \tab access to the subroutine; multiple callers on several memory\par
\tab pages would justify the use of a Page Zero pointer.  By using\par
\tab conditional literals, a proper dynamic decision is made with\par
\tab regard to appropriate generation of subroutine pointers.\par
\par
\tab An implementation detail might specify the use of passed\par
\tab information in the accumulator such as an upper limit on message\par
\tab print length in case the message could exceed some restricted\par
\tab amount.  Alternately, the accumulator could contain flag bits\par
\tab indicating what action to take afterwards (such as displaying\par
\tab\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ).  Other possibilities include the disposition of\par
\tab specially crafted text message strings capable of mixed upper-\par
\tab case and lower-case display.\par
\par
\tab The calling sequence could dictate the string exists inline past \tab the subroutine call; the subroutine should return to just past \tab the word containing the string terminator character.  \tab\par
\par
\tab Alternatively, the address of the string could be passed inline \tab and the subroutine returns to the location following the inline \tab pointer.  If the scope of the subroutine includes extended \tab memory, additional bits may be passed in a variety of ways to \tab form a 15-bit full address of where the string is located within \tab the potential 32K memory space.\par
\par
\tab Not every variation is needed in every situation and all \tab decisions are subject to the programmer's preferences.  As such, \tab source libraries are not only highly specific to a programmer's \tab needs, there are many ways to carry out common functions and a \tab sophisticated programmer may expend effort developing elegant \tab mechanisms to exploit later in future programs.\par
\par
\tab Note: Some features may inherently be somewhat inefficient of\par
\tab memory utilization in exchange for functionality; experienced\par
\tab programmers know what is the most suitable for any given \par
\tab situation.  With experience, additional applications may mandate \tab additional opportunities to expand programming techniques.\par
\par
5)\tab Note: Assuming several uses of a common value within library \tab sections, as long as one usage anywhere in the program is \tab "hardwired" as a Page Zero literal, all related instances will \tab also effectively become Page Zero literals.  In some cases, it \tab may be desirable to include comments about likely combinations \tab within the relevant library routines.\par
\par
  \tab For example, two independent library routines may both use a \tab common literal value; if only one routine is referenced in the \tab main program, the use of actual Page Zero literal references \tab would depend on the usage of a hardwired Page Zero literal in the \tab main code.  If both sections are included in the \tab program, \tab changing any usage to a hardwired Page Zero literal would \tab ensure all other instances would become Page Zero references \tab without regard for the main \tab program code.  Conditional assembly \tab techniques can be applied to elegantly \tab manage situations of this \tab nature.\par
\par
\tab Assume subroutine #1 contains the first potential Page Zero \tab conditional literal; subroutine #2 contains the second potential \tab Page Zero conditional literal.  The programmer could create a \tab mechanism to account for subroutines used in the library such as \tab the following:\par
\par
        SUB1=   1        /SUBROUTINE #1 IS USED IN THIS PROGRAM.\par
\par
        SUB2=   1        /SUBROUTINE #2 IS USED IN THIS PROGRAM.\par
\par
\par
\tab The body of code in the library for subroutine #1 would be \tab bracketed within the following statements.\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB1    <\par
\par
\par
        /       THIS IS WHERE THE ACTUAL SUBROUTINE #1 CODE GOES.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
\tab Similarly for subroutine #2:\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        /       THIS IS WHERE THE ACTUAL SUBROUTINE #2 CODE GOES.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
\par
\tab For the actual conditional literal section in question, \tab management statements similar to the following can implement the \tab example above:\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\cf0\par
\par
\tab (This is the section where the potential Page Zero conditional \tab literal is located within subroutine #1.)\par
\par
        IFZERO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        AND     #77             /REMOVE UPPER SIX BITS.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
        IFNZRO  SUB2    <\par
\par
        AND     [77]           /REMOVE UPPER SIX BITS.\par
\par
                        >\par
\par
\par
\tab The second subroutine uses the conditional literal form of a \tab similar statement; the actual binary code generated within \tab subroutine #2 is a Page Zero literal if a) the main program uses \tab the same Page Zero literal, or b) subroutine #1 uses the explicit \tab Page Zero reference because it was aware of the identical usage \tab within subroutine 2.\par
\par
\tab Future releases of P?S/8 PAL may make mechanisms such as this \tab slightly easier to implement.  There exists an obscure assembler \tab for PDP-8 code including literal support and support for the \tab instructions of the FPP-12 (and FPP-8/A, E).  It was designed and \tab implemented by Jack Burness for use with DIAL-MS (an operating \tab system he wrote exclusively for the 8K PDP-12 with one of a small \tab class of specific disk storage devices).\par
\par
\tab This assembler is known as \i FPPASM\i0  and differs from the embedded \tab DIAL-MS assembler in the following particulars:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab The DIAL-MS assembler supports LINC mode assembly, FPPASM\par
\tab      does not.  (P?S/8 PAL supports LINC mode assembly as a\par
\tab      modular option which can be enabled by the use of\par
\tab      command-line option switches.)\par
\par
\tab b)\tab The DIAL-MS assembler does not support literals of any form, \tab      FPPASM supports Page Zero and current page literals in a\par
\tab      manner generally compatible with TOPS-10 PAL10, OS/8 PAL8\par
\tab      and P?S/8 PAL.\page\cf2 Appendix C Notes (continued)\cf0\par
\par
\tab c)\tab The DIAL-MS assembler and the FPPASM assembler support \tab\tab      certain \tab directives and conditional assembly incompatible\par
\tab      with TOPS-10 PAL10, OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL.  All of the \par
\tab      directives unrelated to conditional assembly represent \par
\tab      alternative syntax for the functionality supported by the \par
\tab      three assemblers mentioned above; \tab in some cases, they are \par
\tab      less powerful and represent older \tab language elements \par
\tab      abandoned in favor of the newer directives.  Any program can \par
\tab      be made compatible merely by performing the \tab appropriate \par
\tab      substitutions as needed.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 PAL supports certain of the FPPASM conditional directives \tab (if certain command-line option switches are set).\par
\par
\tab Additional directives available in FPPASM may be implemented in \tab future releases of P?S/8 PAL which are helpful when supporting \tab source code libraries.  (FPPASM was primarily written to support \tab source code libraries for the FPP-12 hardware.)  (The exact \tab syntax will be made more consistent with the other standard PDP-8 \tab assembler directives; the other conditional directives of the \tab DIAL-MS assembler were added merely for compatibility with source \tab code derived from DIAL-MS.  These directives are not as powerful \tab as the standard PDP-8 assembler analogues as they are more \tab difficult to use and require manual effort unique to these \tab inferior alternatives.)\par
\par
\tab The IFREF directive assembles code contained within angle \tab brackets in a manner consistent with the IFNDEF, IFDEF, IFNZERO \tab and IFZERO directives.  The determinant is whether or not a \tab stated symbol exists in the symbol table.\par
\par
\tab The IFNREF directive assembles code contained within angle \tab brackets if the stated symbol does not exist in the symbol table.\par
\par
\tab While the equivalent management logic can be implemented using \tab IFNDEF and IFNZRO for most source library statements, the IFREF \tab and IFNREF directives can make the implementation slightly easier \tab (especially for the purpose of maintaining source code using \tab conditional literals unique to the P?S/8 PAL assembler).  Called \tab subroutines are assembled because the main program makes \tab reference to them; their inclusion in the effective source code \tab is thus automatic if these directives are used properly.\par
\par
  \tab Note: Library routines can use XLIST directive statements to\par
\tab prevent the presence of unused routines in the assembly listing.  \tab Properly conditionalized, referenced routines will be \tab automatically listed and assembled as \tab needed.\par
\par
