\par
/A\tab If a symbol table or cross-reference is in effect, all symbols \tab will be used.\par
\par
/B   If output files are not specified. the (first) binary output file \tab is % on the system device unit.  See /D and /U.\par
\par
\f0 /C\tab Enable internal reconfiguration of PAL resources to favor symbol \tab table capability over performance.\par
\par
/D\tab If output files are not specified and /B is in effect and a \tab second output file is required, the second output file is $ on \tab the system device unit.  See /B and /U.\par
\par
/E\tab Reset the current page literal extent when leaving the latest\par
\tab page.\par
\par
  \tab Note: This option is ignored unless some combination of the /Q\par
\tab and /O options are set.\par
\f1\par
\f0 /F\tab If PAL chains to the P?S/8 BIN program for the purpose of \tab punching binary paper-tape output, the output device is the high-\tab speed punch.  The default output device is the low-speed punch on\par
\tab either the Teletype Model 33 ASR or Model 35 ASR.\par
\par
/G\tab Chain to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of either loading the \tab\tab\tab binary files created during the assembly or punching binary \tab\tab paper-tapes from the binary files (if the /W option switch is \tab\tab also set).  The chain to P?S/8 BIN is inhibited if there were \tab assembly errors.\par
\par
/H\tab If the /G option is set to chain to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of \tab loading the binary files created by the assembly (and there were \tab no assembly errors) all user memory is preloaded with 7402 (the \tab PDP-8 HLT instruction) before binary file loading.\par
\par
/I\tab Assuming the /G option is set to chain to P?S/8 BIN for the\par
\tab purpose of loading the binary files created by the assembly (and \tab there were no assembly errors) and the non-virtual Slurp loader\par
\tab is in effect, the system device handler will be reloaded after\par
\tab all binary files are loaded.\par
\par
/J\tab Prevent generation of a 12-bit word of 0000 after text \tab\tab\tab strings with an even count of characters as used with the \tab\tab\tab TEXT and SIXBIT directives.\par
\par
/K\tab Enable the change of the title on each page of assembly listing \tab from all input files.  The default is to only obtain the title \tab from the first passed file.\page\cf2\f1 Appendix D (continued)\par
\cf0\f0\par
\tab Note: This assumes listing output is enabled.  If there is \tab neither binary file generation nor listing enabled, force a two \tab pass assembly.\cf2\f1\par
\cf0\f0\par
/L\tab During pass two of the assembly, create listing output.\par
\par
/M\tab Enable chain to P?S/8 MAP to create a bitmap of all binary output \tab generated during the assembly.\par
\par
/N\tab Enable \i neatness\i0  options during an assembly listing.  This \tab includes page numbers and title lines with the current system \tab date and day of the week displayed.\line\line\tab If the listing output is directed to the system console, tear-off \tab lines will be generated\par
\par
/O\tab Enable link generation.  Links will be flagged as assembly errors\par
